---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import { assertion, nodes } from '../lib/feeds'
import { bar, line } from '../lib/chart-spec'

const empty = assertion('the-schools-empty-faster-than-the-county')
const money = assertion('lima-schools-spend-most-and-raise-least')
const poor = assertion('half-the-countys-poor-children-are-in-one-district')
const colleges = assertion('the-county-lost-a-third-of-its-college-students')
const tuition = assertion('in-state-tuition-here-runs-eightfold')
const fields = assertion('the-second-largest-field-of-study-is-not-a-local-one')

const emptySpec = line(empty.figures.map((f) => f.label), [{ name: 'Pupils', y: empty.figures.map((f) => f.value) }], {
  yTitle: 'Pupils enrolled',
  labelLast: true,
})

// Shaped as the renderer reads a bar — `categories` and `values`, not a line's `x` and
// `series`. It was written the other way for one phase and rendered a bar trace with two
// undefined arrays, silently: nothing typechecks a spec, and the table view underneath was
// correct the whole time, so every test passed over an empty chart.
const moneySpec = bar(money.figures.map((f) => f.label), money.figures.map((f) => f.value), {
  yTitle: 'Per cent of revenue',
  tickSuffix: '%',
})

const collegesSpec = bar(
  colleges.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  colleges.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'Per cent of 2010 enrolment lost', tickSuffix: '%' },
)

const tuitionSpec = bar(
  tuition.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  tuition.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'In-state tuition and fees, 2023-24' },
)

// Built from the graph rather than typed out, so the page cannot drift from the corpus.
const districts = nodes
  .filter((n) => n.properties.jurisdiction_type === 'school district')
  .toSorted((a, b) => a.label.localeCompare(b.label))
---

<Base
  title="Schools"
  lede="Twelve school districts hold ground in Allen County. This is what they cost, who governs them, and how fast they are emptying."
>
  <section>
    <h2>Twelve districts, and none of them is the county</h2>
    <p>
      Every other geography on this site closes. Ohio's twelve townships and Lima add up to Allen
      County exactly, in every table the census publishes. The {districts.length} school districts do
      not: five of them hold ground in Putnam, Auglaize, Van Wert or Hancock, and together they
      held 117,057 people in 2023 against a county of 101,685. A figure for "the districts" is not
      a figure for the county, and nothing on this page pretends otherwise.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is a thirteenth school jurisdiction and it is not a district. The educational service
      center is defined by the Revised Code as a county's territory less its city and exempted
      village school districts — so it is Allen County minus Lima City, minus the Allen part of
      Delphos City and minus the Allen part of Bluffton. It has a five-member elected board and no
      boundary of its own.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The schools are emptying twice as fast as the county</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={empty} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="enrolment"
      title="Enrolment in the twelve districts, 2010–2023"
      caption="Fall enrolment from the Census Bureau's annual survey of school system finances, read for every year rather than sampled. Ten of the thirteen year-on-year steps are declines, and one dwarfs the rest: 650 pupils in 2021, where no other year moves by more than 226."
      columns={['Year', 'Pupils']}
      rows={empty.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="enrolment" set:html={JSON.stringify(emptySpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Three districts lost a fifth of their pupils over those thirteen years — Lima at 20.7 per
      cent, Delphos at 20.2 and Waynesfield-Goshen at 20.0. One grew: Bluffton, up 8.8 per cent,
      which is also the cheapest district per pupil and the lowest in child poverty. Allen East lost
      eight pupils in thirteen years.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The district that spends the most raises the least of it</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={money} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="lima-revenue"
      title="Where Lima City Schools' revenue comes from, 2023"
      caption="Every other district in the county raises between 37.0 and 65.8 per cent of its revenue locally. This is compensatory state and federal aid working the way it is designed to."
      columns={['Source', 'Per cent of revenue']}
      rows={money.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="lima-revenue" set:html={JSON.stringify(moneySpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Underneath the shares is the tax base, and underneath the tax base is the housing. Lima raises
      $3,098 a pupil in property tax where Shawnee Local raises $7,727 — a ratio of 2.49, against
      2.19 for the two places' median house values. A district's tax base is what its houses are
      worth, which is the same sorting <a href="/housing">the housing page</a> measures from the
      other end.
    </p>
    <p>
      High spending per pupil has a second cause here that has nothing to do with poverty. The three
      smallest districts — Waynesfield-Goshen at 472 pupils, Pandora-Gilboa at 521 and Columbus
      Grove at 756 — are the second, third and fourth highest spenders in the county, on child
      poverty rates between 6.6 and 10.2 per cent. A school has costs that do not divide, and a
      district of five hundred divides them by five hundred.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Who governs them</h2>
    <p>
      Nine of the twelve boards appear on Allen County's ballot: forty-five people on five-seat
      boards, four-year terms staggered two and three. Five of the forty-five were appointed rather
      than elected to the seat, which is a lighter rate than this county's villages and heavier than
      its townships.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three boards are missing and it is not a gap in retrieval. A school board is elected by its own
      district's voters, and Columbus Grove and Pandora-Gilboa are centred in Putnam County and
      Waynesfield-Goshen in Auglaize, so no Allen County precinct votes on any of them. It is the
      same shape as Delphos's council wards west of the county line, arriving from the other
      direction — a roster is a county's roster, and a jurisdiction that straddles a line is
      partly invisible in it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The poor children are in one district</h2>
    <AssertionCard assertion={poor} />
    <p>
      This is the fourth instrument to describe the same shape. A decennial count, a survey with
      margins, a finance return and now a federal poverty model all say that Lima holds about a
      third of this county's people and about half of whatever is being measured about
      disadvantage. Perry Local is the exception that does not fit either story: 22.1 per cent child
      poverty on 602 children, the county's second-highest rate, in a district whose ground is not
      the township it is named for.
    </p>
    <p>
      One thing this page cannot tell you. 20,444 children aged 5 to 17 live in these twelve
      districts and 16,113 are enrolled in them. Some of the difference is that the two figures are
      different instruments; the rest is private and parochial schools, community schools, home
      instruction and open enrolment, none of which this corpus has ever recorded. It is an open
      question, and it is the first one here that came from putting two of the corpus's own sources
      beside each other.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>And what happens after the twelfth grade</h2>
    <p>
      Six post-secondary institutions have addresses in Allen County, and until this phase this
      corpus had a node for one of them. They enrol 8,615 students in the autumn and 10,789 across
      the twelve months, and award 2,918 credentials a year — of which 2,482, or 85.1 per cent, are
      below a bachelor's degree. The county grants 1,110 associate's degrees and 1,106 certificates
      of a year or less against 375 bachelor's degrees and 58 master's.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={colleges} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="college-fall"
      title="Enrolment lost since 2010, by institution"
      caption="IPEDS twelve-month unduplicated headcount, 2010 against 2023. The county's own population fell 3.9 per cent between its 2010 and 2020 censuses; two thirds of this fall was complete before 2020."
      columns={['Institution', 'Per cent of 2010 enrolment lost']}
      rows={colleges.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value.toFixed(1)])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="college-fall" set:html={JSON.stringify(collegesSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      The largest is Rhodes State College, at 3,968 students — of whom 615 are full-time and 3,353
      are not, so a college of four thousand is the size of two thousand. It shares 4240 Campus
      Drive with Ohio State's Lima campus, in Bath Township outside the city both are named for,
      and the Census Bureau puts the two in the same block. A third pair share a block as well: the
      Ohio State Beauty Academy stands where the University of Northwestern Ohio does, and IPEDS
      records it as a member of that university's system.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={fields} />
    <p>
      The county's largest field of study is health professions, at 757 awards, which agrees with a
      county whose largest employer is health care. Its second is mechanic and repair technologies,
      at 706 — every one of them from the University of Northwestern Ohio, which draws 582 of its
      877 entering students from outside Ohio, most of them from Michigan, Indiana, Pennsylvania and
      New York. The county's other two institutions that report the question draw 99.1 and 97.0 per
      cent of their entering students from Ohio.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the second-largest thing this county's colleges do is not a fact about this county's
      workforce, and this site says so in the same breath as the number. That is a rule now.
    </p>
    <AssertionCard assertion={tuition} />
    <ChartFigure
      id="tuition"
      title="Tuition and fees for an Ohio resident, 2023-24"
      caption="IPEDS academic-year charges. Apollo Career Center and the Ohio State Beauty Academy price by programme rather than by year and are not in this table. Out of state, Rhodes State charges $9,120 and Ohio State-Lima $34,443; the two private institutions charge everyone the same."
      columns={['Institution', 'Tuition and fees']}
      rows={tuition.figures.map((f) => [f.label, '$' + f.value.toLocaleString('en-US')])}
    />
    <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="tuition" set:html={JSON.stringify(tuitionSpec)} is:inline />
    <p>
      Only two of the six provide housing at all — Bluffton University with 780 beds and the
      University of Northwestern Ohio with 1,684. Bluffton has had more beds than students for
      years: 838 beds and 1,292 students in 2010, 780 and 678 now. Neither college on the shared
      campus east of Lima has ever reported a bed, which leaves the 104 people the 2020 census
      counted in student housing half a kilometre south of it unexplained.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
